{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1520308047,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzMDI1NTQxMjA=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2269","origin":"github"},"title":"Interface: Positional argument with dashes leads to an error","message":"When converting the addurls plugin into a command, I wanted to use a positional argument that had a dash in it, but this doesn't seem to be possible.\n\nAs a simple example (which can be applied to master):\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/datalad/interface/__init__.py b/datalad/interface/__init__.py\nindex bd9019d36..cf2eff674 100644\n--- a/datalad/interface/__init__.py\n+++ b/datalad/interface/__init__.py\n@@ -68,6 +68,7 @@\n         ('datalad.interface.download_url', 'DownloadURL', 'download-url'),\n         ('datalad.interface.run', 'Run', 'run'),\n         ('datalad.interface.rerun', 'Rerun', 'rerun'),\n+        ('datalad.interface.posarg', 'Posarg', 'posarg'),\n     ])\n\n _group_plumbing = (\ndiff --git a/datalad/interface/posarg.py b/datalad/interface/posarg.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 000000000..6147b8f3b\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/datalad/interface/posarg.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@\n+\"\"\"Take a position argument\"\"\"\n+\n+__docformat__ = 'restructuredtext'\n+\n+from datalad.distribution.dataset import datasetmethod\n+from datalad.interface.base import Interface\n+from datalad.interface.base import build_doc\n+from datalad.interface.utils import eval_results\n+from datalad.support.param import Parameter\n+\n+import logging\n+\n+lgr = logging.getLogger('datalad.interface.posarg')\n+\n+\n+@build_doc\n+class Posarg(Interface):\n+    \"\"\"Blah\"\"\"\n+    _params_ = dict(\n+        pos_arg=Parameter(\n+            args=(\"pos-arg\",),\n+            doc=\"a positional argument.  Try to use me.\"),\n+        opt_arg=Parameter(\n+            args=(\"--opt-arg\",),\n+            doc=\"an optional argument\"),\n+    )\n+\n+    @staticmethod\n+    @datasetmethod(name='posarg')\n+    @eval_results\n+    def __call__(pos_arg, opt_arg=None):\n+        print(posarg)\n+        yield {}\n\n```\n\nInvoking this results in the following error:\n\n```\n$ datalad posarg ok\n[ERROR  ] 'Namespace' object has no attribute 'pos_arg' [base.py:\u003cdictcomp\u003e:393] (AttributeError)\n```\n\nIf I replace `args=(\"pos-arg\",)` with `args=(\"pos_arg\",)`, I see the same error.\n\nIf I instead add `dest=\"pos_arg\"`, I get a different error:\n\n```\n$ datalad posarg ok\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/datalad/venv-datalad/bin/datalad\", line 8, in \u003cmodule\u003e\n    main()\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/datalad/datalad/cmdline/main.py\", line 304, in main\n    parser = setup_parser(args or sys.argv)\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/datalad/datalad/cmdline/main.py\", line 245, in setup_parser\n    _intf.setup_parser(subparser)\n  File \"/home/kyle/src/python/datalad/datalad/interface/base.py\", line 375, in setup_parser\n    **parser_kwargs)\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.5/argparse.py\", line 1320, in add_argument\n    raise ValueError('dest supplied twice for positional argument')\nValueError: dest supplied twice for positional argument\n```\n\n#### First error\n\nI think what is going on is that `base.Interface.setup_parser` assumes that argparse replaces all dashes in the argument's name with underscores.  However, it seems that `ArgumentParser.parse_known_args` [leaves][0] dashes in positional arguments untouched:\n\n```\nNamespace(_=False, cfg_overrides=None, change_path=None, common_debug=False, common_idebug=False, common_on_failure=None, common_output_format='default', common_report_status=None, common_report_type=None, common_run_after=None, common_run_before=None, func=\u003cbound method Interface.call_from_parser of \u003cclass 'datalad.interface.posarg.Posarg'\u003e\u003e, help=None, log_level='warning', logger=\u003clogging.Logger object at 0x7f07c37babe0\u003e, opt_arg=None, pbs_runner=None, pos-arg='ok', subparser=ArgumentParser(prog='datalad posarg', usage=None, description='Blah', formatter_class=\u003cclass 'argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter'\u003e, conflict_handler='error', add_help=False))\n```\n\nNote the \"pos-arg\".  So, when `base.Interface.call_from_parser` [goes looking][1] for `pos_arg`, it doesn't find it, leading to the attribute error.\n\nOr, to remove this from the context of datalad:\n\n```python\n\u003e\u003e\u003e import argparse\n\u003e\u003e\u003e parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n\u003e\u003e\u003e parser.add_argument('fo-o')\n\u003e\u003e\u003e parser.add_argument('--ba-z')\n\u003e\u003e\u003e parser.parse_args('arg'.split())\nNamespace(ba_z=None, fo-o='arg')\n```\n\n#### Second error\n\nThe second error seems to come from the fact that `argparse.add_argument` treats `dest` [differently][2] depending on whether the argument is a positional argument or an optional one, but `Interface.base.setup_parser` doesn't account for this.\n\nFixing this error would make it easy to work around the first error by specifying `dest`.  If I'm thinking about this correctly, it should be enough for `setup_parser` to check whether the argument is a positional one and, if so, move `dest` from `parser_kwargs` to the first argument in `parser_args`.\n\n[0]: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/afe3db7e9fc01d84947452e33f8786cb953420cb/datalad/cmdline/main.py#L312\n[1]: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/afe3db7e9fc01d84947452e33f8786cb953420cb/datalad/interface/base.py#L393\n[2]: https://docs.python.org/2/library/argparse.html?highlight=add_argument#dest","files":null}]}